Imported: src/ and vt/ and LICENSE. Left out: the upstream tools, trace interposers, test streams, benchmark data and
documentation (about 100 MB), none of which the library needs.

Changes made here, in this order of importance:
  vt/nvencvt.c   NVEncVT_RegisterDecoders(): registers only the decoders, for the decode service (appended before
                 load_conf).
                 EnableLowLatencyRateControl: declared as a selection property of the H.264 and HEVC encoders, accepted by
                 SetProperty / CopyProperty and listed among the supported properties. A session that sets it is real-time
                 with no B pictures. Without the declaration VideoToolbox drops the encoder from the candidates of any
                 client that asks for low-latency rate control (FFmpeg's low_delay, Sunshine), and answers -12902.

  src/hevcparse.c, src/h264parse.c, src/av1parse.c  Fixes found by running the parsers under AddressSanitizer with a coverage-guided
                 fuzzer (tools/test_nvenc_parsers.py holds one case for each; to be sent upstream):
                 - ue() clamps to 2^28: a code up to 2^32 - 2 became a negative int, passed "id > max" checks and indexed the SPS / PPS
                   arrays (an out-of-bounds write when the parameter set was stored);
                 - scaling_list_data(): pred_matrix_id_delta checked against 0..matrixId before it indexes the list array;
                 - st_ref_pic_set(): the inter-predicted lists were written past 32 entries before the limit was checked;
                 - hrd_parameters(): cpb_cnt_minus1 limited to 31 (a stream that had ended still ran 2^28 loop rounds);
                 - hevc_parse_sps() / h264_parse_sps(): the sizes, bit depths and block sizes of the specification's ranges are
                   checked before the SPS is stored (16 * width overflowed int); av1 decode_unsigned_subexp_with_ref() shifts unsigned.

  Blackwell and the other Turing chips (tools/test_nvencvt_blackwell.py), written from public sources only:
                 - src/rm.c: Blackwell's classes 0xcfb7, 0xcfb0, 0xc96f (open-gpu-kernel-modules clcfb7.h, clcfb0.h, clc96f.h);
                 - src/blocklinear.c: gob_offset / gob_untile_plane / gob_layout_for, the Blackwell8Bit and Blackwell16Bit plane
                   layouts and the rule that picks one by element size (Mesa NIL, src/nouveau/nil/tiling.rs and copy.rs, MIT);
                 - src/dec.c: the H.264 decoder untiles in the card's layout;
                 - src/channel.c: methods that do not fit the push buffer are dropped and the submission fails (it wrote past
                   the mapping when a channel stopped fetching); the buffer is reused after a completion the decoder saw,
                   because clc96f.h documents no GPGet word;
                 - vt/: card kinds for Blackwell (H.264 decoder only) and TU10x / TU116 (decoders only), by PCI device ID.
                   The ID is asked of the card's display function only (PCI base class 3). Upstream took the first NVIDIA
                   function whose ID is in a known block, and the audio functions of RTX 40 cards, of most RTX 50 cards and
                   of the RTX 30 cards below the 3080 have IDs in the GA10x block (0x228b-0x22ec). Listed before the GPU,
                   one made an RTX 50 a GA10x card, which skipped the self-test below and registered the VP9 decoder, and
                   took the encoders from an RTX 40 (to be sent upstream).
                 Not run on a card yet. On TU10x / TU116 nothing registers unless NVMTL_NVENCVT names the process or is 1.
                 On Blackwell the plugin asks for the library in the decode service (and where NVMTL_NVENCVT says so), and
                 the library registers nothing there until the self-test below has passed in that process.

  The Blackwell H.264 decode self-test (tools/test_h264_kat.c, test_h264_kat_selftest.py, test_h264_kat_vectors.py); the
  new files are written here and are under this directory's licence:
                 - src/h264kat_core.h: the frame hash (FNV-1a 64 over the cropped luma rows and the chroma rows), the match of
                   a stream's frames against the expected ones as a set, and the verdict line. Plain C;
                 - src/h264kat.c, h264kat.h: decodes the embedded streams with the calls a VideoToolbox session makes
                   (h264dec_open / _nal / _flush / _pop / _read_nv12 / _release) and requires every expected frame once,
                   none besides, no macroblock or engine error, within five seconds. On a wrong frame it reads the surface
                   again in the other plane layouts and says whether one of them fits; it never changes how the decoder
                   reads. Run once per process; one line in the system log either way;
                 - src/h264kat_vectors.h: the two streams (320x180 with B pictures, two slices and three references;
                   1920x1080 I P P) and the hashes of their 11 frames. Generated by tools/gen_h264_kat.py from ffmpeg's
                   synthetic sources testsrc2 and gradients, encoded with libx264; the header records the command lines and
                   the encoder version. SEI and access unit delimiters are removed, so the bytes are parameter sets and
                   slices and carry no encoder banner and nobody's picture. The hashes are those of ffmpeg's decoder on the
                   same bytes (H.264 decoding is exact by specification), not of this library;
                 - vt/nvencvt.c: NVEncVT_Register and NVEncVT_RegisterDecoders return kVTCouldNotFindVideoDecoderErr and
                   register nothing on Blackwell until that test has passed in the process.
                   NVEncVT_BlackwellSelfTest, an exported constant, marks a build that does this: the plugin looks it up
                   and leaves a library without it alone on Blackwell, so that a newer plugin beside an older library
                   cannot put the untested decoder into the decode service. build/build_nvencvt.sh lists it among the
                   five symbols the library may export.

Where upstream's own facts come from (read before calling this library clean of NVIDIA's closed driver): the decoders'
picture setups are open-gpu-doc's nvdec_drv.h. The ENCODERS' per-picture control blocks (src/h264enctmpl.h,
hevcenctmpl.h, av1enctmpl.h) are byte tables that upstream generated from traces of NVIDIA's Linux driver encoding on
the same cards, and src/enc.c, la.h and two decoder comments cite such traces for buffer sizes and method use; the
input layout in blocklinear.h was measured from a working client's surface. That is observation of a driver's output,
not disassembly, but it is the same kind of source as NullMoth's own encoder libraries.

Build inputs (fetched at build time by build/build_nvencvt.sh, not vendored):
  NVIDIA open-gpu-kernel-modules 610.57.04   RM API headers, MIT. The kernel module's RM is 620.70.06: the build compares
                 every struct and constant the library takes from these headers with the tree the kernel module is built
                 from (tools/nvencvt_header_abi.py) and stops on a difference. The one it lets pass, with its reason, is
                 in that file: 620 no longer reports a mapping's caching type in NVOS33's flags.
